        "thinking": "Let me set up this problem. Let's say I buy:\n- p pens at 3 each\n- n notebooks at 7 each\n\nConstraints:\n- 3p + 7n = 50\n- p ≥ 1\n- n ≥ 1\n\nI want to maximize p.\n\nFrom the equation: 3p + 7n = 50\nSo: p = (50 - 7n)/3\n\nFor p to be a whole number, (50 - 7n) must be divisible by 3.\n\nLet me check: 50 ≡ 2 (mod 3) and 7 ≡ 1 (mod 3)\nSo 50 - 7n ≡ 2 - n (mod 3)\n\nFor this to be divisible by 3: 2 - n ≡ 0 (mod 3)\nSo n ≡ 2 (mod 3)\n\nSince n ≥ 1, possible values are: n = 2, 5, 8, 11, ...\n\nIf n = 2: p = (50 - 14)/3 = 36/3 = 12 ✓\nIf n = 5: p = (50 - 35)/3 = 15/3 = 5 ✓\nIf n = 8: p = (50 - 56)/3 = -6/3 = -2 ✗ (negative)\n\nSo the maximum p is 12 when n = 2.\n\nLet me verify: 3(12) + 7(2) = 36 + 14 = 50 ✓\n\nAnd we have at least one of each: p = 12 ≥ 1 and n = 2 ≥ 1 ✓",
